00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:08.540 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  recent advancements in technology have  allowed animators to push the limits of  their work resulting in more creative  and detailed films and TV shows than  ever before to find out how six of these  stunning and unique animations were  created let's take a look behind the  scenes stop-motion animated movies have  been around for over 100 years  but the production house like estudios  brought this animation style into the  21st century by utilizing 3d printing a  technique that is especially prevalent  in their 2016 masterpiece Kubo and the  two strings  traditionally stop-motion animated  characters had to be made of a moldable  material such as clay so that the faces  of the characters could be changed  however by using 3d printed faces Leica  Studios is able to alter the facial  expressions of its characters more  quickly and consistently  each expression is created using 3d  modeling software the models are then  printed with the 3d printer and magnets  are added to the back of the face so  they can later be attached to the  puppets there were approximately 48  million different combinations of facial  expressions created for the film's main  character kuba his face consists of a  top and bottom faceplate that combine  the tiny scene between the two pieces  was painted out in post-production  because there were so many faces each  face had a code on the back of it in  order to keep all of them organized  advanced 3d printing technology also  allowed Kubo in the two strings to be  able to create intricate puppets that  would be impossible to create otherwise  such as the character called moon beast  who was like his first completely 3d  printed character moon beast was made of  881 individual 3d printed parts 130  color parts and 751 components for the  armatures used in its body and legs  [Music]  Loving Vincent is the first  feature-length animated film in which  every frame was hand-painted using oil  on canvas approximately 65,000 frames in  total the film was painted in the style  of Vincent van Gogh featuring 94 of the  artists original paintings as the  settings for the story in order to  create the film the production team  invented a special animation process  first they filmed actors on a blue stage  with background elements digitally added  later then one of the 125 highly skilled  artists on the team painted over each  frame of this live-action footage in the  style of Van Gogh the paintings were  then photographed in a symbol to  complete the animation process the  producers faced several challenges in  this ambitious project first they had to  change the color palette of several of  Van Gogh's paintings because while van  Gogh painted across all four seasons  the film's plot takes place only in the  summer  additionally van Gogh experimented with  several different styles throughout his  life  so characters had to be painted  differently based on which van Gogh  painting was being used as the backdrop  when the production team realized they  needed to hire more painters to finish  the project on time they discovered that  they're literally weren't enough trained  painters available in Poland where the  film was being made so they posted a job  listing and ended up hiring 45 oil  painters from around the world sifting  through 5,000 applications given all of  these factors it's no surprise that it  took the team about five years to  complete the project the film's director  said that they had without a doubt  invented the slowest form of filmmaking  ever devised  it took producers over a year to create  just 10 seconds of test animation  footage that they were happy with for  spider-man into the spider-verse the  eye-grabbing animation style of the 2018  film looks wildly different from typical  animated movies and it borrows many of  its innovative animation  techniques from comic books one of the  most noticeable differences involves  framerate animated films are typically  24 frames per second and creating a  different image for each frame is known  as animating on once spider-verse broke  the mold and animated much of the movie  on tubes meaning that they kept some  images on screen for two frames each  character's pose lasts longer and is  much more pronounced they alternated  between on twos and on ones depending on  the nature of the scene another defining  characteristic of the film is that there  isn't any motion blur a CGI trick that  most new films use to soften a movement  and make it seem more real instead they  used an old-school technique called a  smear this was used a lot in early  cartoons to create the sense of motion  if you look at a single frame you'll  notice things like multiple limbs to  create the illusion of movement the  producers also decided not to create a  traditional depth of field by using lens  blurs and instead utilized mists  printing inspired by the common error in  comic books when ink is misaligned  when something is in focus the colors  aligned in our crisper while the colors  of background objects are often offset  [Music]  the characters in Netflix's  oscar-nominated film Klaus look  three-dimensional but unlike most  animated movies these days the  characters in Klaus aren't all CGI and  can't even be considered 3d the  characters were first hand drawn inked  and painted using digital tablets then  the artists were able to paint with  light  thanks to software created specifically  for Klaus the second major feature that  helped bring the characters to life and  added to the 3d illusion was texturing  with another piece of software they used  contour lighting and motion to add  surface qualities to specific parts of a  character for example the skin could  appear smooth while the clothing would  be rougher in the end the characters  appeared much more 3d and immersed in  their and VY  as opposed to looking like stickers on  top of an elaborately painted background  while the majority of the film followed  this 2d process the animators did use 3d  models for some characters and objects  and combine the two styles seamlessly  even though these were created using CGI  they were lit the same way as the 2d  characters work  scenes like this chase scene at the end  of the film so seamlessly combined 2d  and 3d elements that it can be difficult  to tell which is which  while audiences and critics were divided  on Disney's 2019 Lion King remake it's  undeniable that the animation was a  technical triumph as it's the most  photorealistic animated major motion  picture to date one of the first steps  in most animation films is  pre-visualization where an extremely low  resolution version of the animation is  created in order to perfect character  movements and camera angles  however The Lion King took a new  approach to this phase creating  simplified animated sequences that could  be viewed in virtual reality the  director and production team would then  put on VR headsets which allow them to  actually step foot in the virtual set  that they had created this allowed them  more freedom to explore each scene and  let them easily set up shots adjust  lighting and choreograph movements this  digital environment helped them emulate  the nature documentary aesthetic that  they were aiming for the Lion King  animation team also ran into the unique  challenge of having to make the animals  not only realistic but also able to sing  and talk in order to pull this off they  time the characters breathing to their  dialogue and would make the belly  muscles and diaphragm tighten that way  it felt like the animals were forcing  air out of their mouths as they spoke  each layer of these animals also went  through a simulation pass an additional  phase of animation where skin details  such as muscle ripples skin wrinkles and  fur interaction are all added striking  that balance between staying true to the  original film  and creating a hyper-realistic Lee  animated Lion King for a new audience  was a particularly tough challenge and  it required 77 million hours of  animation rendering the 2019 Amazon  Prime series undone was created using  rotoscoping the process of drawing  animations over live-action video  footage rotoscoping has been around  since the early 1900s but the  rotoscoping process for undone is so  much more advanced that it's practically  unrecognizable from its predecessors the  producers for undone were originally  going to film the series using  live-action but they ultimately decided  that using rotoscope animation would  give the series the surreal feeling that  it necessitated as the story revolves RN  a young woman who has an unsteady grip  on reality the scenes were initially  shot with actors and minimal sets using  grids on set as reference points for the  animators there was no need for  green-screen because every area of the  frames would be painted over during the  rotoscoping process the live-action was  then rotoscoped  with animators drawing crisp lines over  the footage the rotoscope animation was  then sent to Amsterdam where artists  would use oil-based paints to add colors  and shadows to the footage while many of  the backgrounds were painted in  Amsterdam using oil on canvas some  scenes required backgrounds that were  created using 3d modeling software to  maintain the uniform painted look the 3d  animation team would send a layout to  the painters who would paint over the  image after the painting was complete  the 3d artists could then use the  finished painting as a reference for  texturing the 3d environment  what film do you think we should have  included on the list  what animated film would you like us to  discuss next let us know in the comments  [Music]

